course: dictionary
lesson: 14608
title: A college for skills: polytechnic
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: When you meet a new word, read the words around it. They are its context, and they leave clues. «Mia wants to go to the polytechnic to learn how to build bridges.» Building bridges is a practical, skilled job, so a polytechnic must be a place where you learn skills.
explain: «My cousin studies computing at the local poly.» Poly is a short, everyday name for a polytechnic, so when you see poly, think of the same kind of college. Ask yourself: what kind of place teaches computing and building? Then try your meaning in the sentence to see if it still makes sense.
explain: The word also works as an adjective. «The school offers a polytechnic course» means a course that teaches many technical subjects. Poly means many and technic means skill, so the word itself points to many skilled subjects.
fact: A polytechnic is a college that teaches many practical and technical subjects.
fact: Subjects at a polytechnic include engineering, building, computing and design.
fact: Polytechnics often teach older students and adults.
fact: In the UK, most polytechnics became universities in 1992.
fact: «Poly» is a short name for a polytechnic in the UK, New Zealand and elsewhere.
exercise: «Mia wants to go to the polytechnic to learn how to build bridges.» What is a polytechnic? | A kind of bridge | *A college that teaches many practical and technical subjects | A shop that sells tools | A park with a big lake
exercise: «My cousin studies computing at the local poly.» What does poly mean here? | A type of bread | A loud noise | *A short name for polytechnic | A kind of pet
exercise: «The school is a polytechnic, teaching engineering and design.» Find and copy the word that describes a school teaching many technical subjects. | *polytechnic | political | polite | pottery
test: Which subjects might a polytechnic teach? | Gardening only | Only swimming | *Building and design | Reading poems
test: In the UK, what happened to most polytechnics in 1992? | *They became universities | They were sold to shops | They all became parks | They turned into farms
exam: Who is most likely to study at a polytechnic? | *An adult learning a skilled job | A captain who never leaves port | A baby learning to talk | A dog
